{% from 'ingressroute.yml.j2' import ingressroute with context %}
---
{#
  The UI route, plus the strangler-bridge route once unsuffixed_hostname is set. /webhook/ needs no
  route of its own here: the bridge route the macro emits carries no Authelia at all, and the
  Docker edge's half of the bypass is generated from bridge_bypass_prefixes. That pairing is
  what reproduces the compose template's `n8n-webhook` router — see daniel-box.yml.
#}
{{ ingressroute(
    container_item.name,
    container_item.hostname | default(container_item.name),
    container_item.port,
    container_item.use_authelia,
    bypass_prefixes=container_item.bridge_bypass_prefixes | default([])
  )
}}
{#
  There was an `n8n-monitoring` route here until 2026-08-16: a rate-limited-only door onto
  /api/v1/workflows and /api/v1/executions, admitted so monitor-bridge's check_n8n could poll
  n8n from daniel-server. That caller moved in-cluster on 2026-08-14 and now reaches n8n over
  the Service DNS name, leaving the route with no known caller.

  The route's own comment demanded a caller census before removal, because "looks unused" is
  how two of three past allowlist narrowings broke a real caller. That census was run on
  2026-08-16: `traefik_router_requests_total{router=~".*n8n-monitoring.*"}` returned zero
  series across the full 30-day Prometheus retention — not zero requests recently, no series at
  all. So it was deleted rather than narrowed.

  It mattered because /api/v1 is READ-WRITE — it can create, modify, activate and delete
  workflows — and this was the one route in the fleet reaching it with neither Authelia nor a
  ClientIP matcher in front. X-N8N-API-KEY was the only gate.

  If a future caller needs this again, add it through the shared monitoring_route() macro so it
  inherits the ClientIP matcher, rather than reintroducing a hand-rolled twin.
#}
